The project must develop a result from an eligible earlier EU project further, in any field, and work on technology and business in parallel.
Mandatory:
- further technology development of results from an eligible earlier project (EIC Pathfinder or its predecessors, ERC Proof of Concept, Horizon 2020 or Horizon Europe Pillar II or European Defence Fund Research and Innovation Actions, or Horizon Europe Research Infrastructures actions);
- user-centric methods with learning from early customer or user feedback;
- business readiness work: market research, value proposition, business plan and business model, intellectual property protection, and regulation, certification and standardisation;
- a stated path to market and milestones with verifiable KPIs (key performance indicators).
Optional: forming and spinning out a new company as part of the project.
Excluded: results already at TRL 5 or beyond; results of earlier EIC Transition projects; results from projects funded through financial support to third parties; and, as not recommended, niche or very small markets. Business planning, market validation and customer engagement should not be subcontracted.

TenneT would be most credible as launching customer or end user signing the Letter of Intent and offering a test setting and data.
This is an interpretation, based on the mandatory Letter of Intent and the call's mention of pledged infrastructure, data and personnel. TenneT could:
- back a grid-relevant result coming from a university or start-up, for example from Delft University of Technology, with which it runs the digital twin facility NFDTES;
- offer that facility, grid data or a pilot location for validation in the application environment;
- give operational feedback during user testing and help validate the business model as a first customer.
The commitment must be real and checkable, as the agency may verify it.
